The problem asks us to count the number of "Snake numbers" in a given range $[L, R]$.
A Snake number is a positive integer $\ge 10$ such that its most significant digit (MSD) is strictly larger than every other digit in the number.
The constraints are $10 \le L \le R \le 10^{18}$.

Let $N$ be a number. Let $d_k d_{k-1} \dots d_1 d_0$ be its decimal representation, where $d_k$ is the MSD.
The condition is $d_k > d_i$ for all $i \in \{0, 1, \dots, k-1\}$.
This means $d_k$ must be the unique maximum digit in the number.
Since $d_k$ is the MSD, it cannot be 0 (unless the number is 0, but the problem says positive integer $\ge 10$).
Also, since $d_k > d_i$ for all other digits, $d_k$ must be at least 1 (since digits are $0-9$). Actually, since $d_k > d_i$, if there is any other digit, $d_k$ must be strictly greater than 0.
The digits $d_i$ for $i < k$ can be anything from $0$ to $d_k - 1$.

Let's denote the function $Count(X)$ as the number of Snake numbers in the range $[1, X]$.
Then the answer for the range $[L, R]$ will be $Count(R) - Count(L-1)$.

So the problem reduces to calculating $Count(X)$ for a given $X$.
$X$ can be up to $10^{18}$, so we need an algorithm with complexity related to the number of digits, which is small (up to 18 or 19). Digit DP or combinatorics is suitable.

